[38;2;124;124;124mbounded variation on compact intervals), then the quadratic variation $[X]$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists and equals zero.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof: For almost every $\omega$, the path $t \mapsto X_t(\omega)$ is $C^1$. Fix[0m[38;2;124;124;124msuch an $\omega$ and a compact interval $[0,T]$. Let $\pi = \{0 = t_0 < t_1 < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cdots < t_n = T\}$ be a partition with mesh $\|\pi\| = \max_i |t_{i+1} - t_i|$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$S(\pi) = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (X_{t_{i+1}} - X_{t_i})^2$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the mean value theorem, $X_{t_{i+1}} - X_{t_i} = X'_{s_i}(\omega) \cdot [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(t_{i+1} - t_i)$ for some $s_i \in (t_i, t_{i+1})$. So:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$S(\pi) = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (X'_{s_i}(\omega))^2 (t_{i+1} - t_i)^2 \leq \|\pi\| [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\cdot \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (X'_{s_i}(\omega))^2 (t_{i+1} - t_i)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe sum $\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (X'_{s_i}(\omega))^2 (t_{i+1} - t_i)$ is a Riemann sum[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfor $\int_0^T (X'_s(\omega))^2 ds$, which converges to that integral (finite [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msince $X'$ is continuous on $[0,T]$). And $\|\pi\| \to 0$. So $S(\pi) \to 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince this holds for a.e. $\omega$, the quadratic variation is zero almost [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msurely.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, no smooth stochastic process can have nonzero quadratic variation.[0m

